WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.843
How do you find the genes that let an axon cross only once?

2
00:00:07.723 --> 00:00:09.363
Look at the nerve cord of a fly embryo.

3
00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:13.763
Stained for its axons, the nerve cord looks like a ladder.

4
00:00:14.683 --> 00:00:16.563
Two long rails run the length of the body.

5
00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:20.207
In each segment, two rungs cross between them.

6
00:00:21.047 --> 00:00:22.347
These rungs are the commissures.

7
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:28.247
Most commissural axons cross once, turn, and run in a rail on the far side.

8
00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:35.067
In 1993, Corey Goodman's laboratory reported a screen for mutations that disturb

9
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:36.067
this ladder.

10
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:41.507
The screen recovered loss-of-function mutants, in which a gene's activity is reduced

11
00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:44.187
or lost. These were strong losses.

12
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:47.157
Before looking, set up the logic.

13
00:00:48.077 --> 00:00:49.597
The midline makes a repellent.

14
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:52.177
Axons carry a receptor for it.

15
00:00:52.957 --> 00:00:55.457
Something in the axon sets when the receptor works.

16
00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:01.150
First case. Axons lose their main receptor for the repellent.

17
00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.150
What does the ladder look like?

18
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:11.323
Little holds axons off the midline.

19
00:01:12.163 --> 00:01:13.463
Ipsilateral axons cross.

20
00:01:14.543 --> 00:01:16.683
Commissural axons cross and then cross back.

21
00:01:17.563 --> 00:01:18.563
The rungs thicken.

22
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:22.017
This is the mutant called roundabout, or robo.

23
00:01:22.877 --> 00:01:24.517
Its axons circle back across.

24
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:29.257
Second case. The midline makes no repellent.

25
00:01:29.977 --> 00:01:30.977
Predict the ladder.

26
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:41.377
The repellent's push is gone, so axons that reach the midline are not pushed out.

27
00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:43.597
They enter and do not leave.

28
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.480
Over time the two rails collapse into a single bundle down the middle.

29
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.360
This is the slit mutant.

30
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:56.593
Third case. Commissural axons have a working receptor before they cross.

31
00:01:57.573 --> 00:01:58.573
What now?

32
00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:06.067
They are pushed away before they can cross.

33
00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:10.027
Almost no axon crosses, and the rungs nearly vanish.

34
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:12.957
This is commissureless, or comm.

35
00:02:13.797 --> 00:02:16.057
The name says it: the rungs are nearly gone.

36
00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:21.487
In 1998, Thomas Kidd, Tear, Goodman and colleagues cloned robo.

37
00:02:22.527 --> 00:02:25.247
It encodes a receptor that spans the membrane once.

38
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.110
Its timing was the telling part.

39
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.790
Axons that never cross carry Robo on their growth cones from the start.

40
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:38.280
Axons that do cross carry high levels of Robo only after they have crossed.

41
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.560
Picture the ladder stained for Robo protein.

42
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:43.460
The rails are bright.

43
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.660
They hold ipsilateral axons and axons already across.

44
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.730
The rungs, where axons are crossing, are much dimmer.

45
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:57.510
The same axon carries little Robo while it crosses, and a lot once it is across.

46
00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.290
That is what deaf until crossed means.

47
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.490
Deaf here means a much weaker response, not none.
